  "input_contract": {
    "seagallop": "DoSeagallopFerryScene API invocation; callback1 NULL, source/destination inputs 11; successful 2048-byte tilemap allocation and valid graphics resources. Real setter initializes state0, all eight setup states execute, real ResetTasks/CreateTask/RunTasks produce callback and timer140. Every external fade starts active and becomes inactive only after 17 conditional UpdatePaletteFade calls.",
    "shockwave": "At processing of the structurally parsed command+65 of actual registered move351 script. LaunchBattleAnimation whole execution and earlier graphics commands are not claimed. Script context is taken from real table/prefix/command; no task selector, task state or internal timer is injected. A valid empty task list, attacker0/target1, visual count0, frames-to-wait0 and script-active1 are finite context conditions. Real opcode dispatch reads real function/priority/no-argument fields; real CreateTask zeroes data and registers callback. Actual next delay12 command executes and the dispatcher returns with balanced stack before the scheduler is called.",
    "sprite_failure": "All 43 CreateSprite calls return MAX_SPRITES=64 under an explicit allocation-failure condition. Real compare skips every sprite/data3 increment, real coordinate and segment writers reach segment5/state3, then real zero data3 branch reaches BL. Natural battle, successful sprite lifecycle and resource exhaustion reachability are not claimed.",
    "abi": "All opaque boundaries return normally preserving ABI callee-saved registers and only concrete future-read-before-write memory fields. All other RAM is actually erased during replay. Outputs and preservation are separate; the same task/main/tilemap epochs at their live uses are conditions, not universal effects.",
    "minimum": "Only complete Thumb instructions covering the two inventory windows: ShockWave BL+static B = 6 bytes; Seagallop two adjacent complete BLs = 8 bytes. Execution stops before the first hit call. No literal/data/padding/whole function classification."
